[What is the gradient of a node
 [Suppose the loss function is {L} (depending on a bunch of things)]
 [The gradient of the node {n} is {grad(n) = frac(d L, d n)}]
]

[What would happen to {L} if you tweak {n} by {h * grad(n)},
 where {h} is some small amount
 [I guess {L} would move by {h * grad(n) * grad(n)}
  and what the heck is that?]
 [If {h} is positive, then {h * grad(n) * grad(n)} is positive, too,
  so {L} would go up]]

[Learn about exponentiation
 [;integer_power_rule If {n} is a positive integer, and {b} is anything
  {b^n = b*b*...*b}, the product has {n} {b} in it]
 
 [From @integer_power_rule we have the ;integer_add_power_rule
  {b^(n+m) = b^n * b^m}]
 
 [Then {b^0 = 1}]
 
 [;negation_of_power What would {b^(-n)} be?
  {
   b^(-n) * b^n = b^0 = 1
   -> b^(-n) = frac(1, b^n)
  }
  So it's the inverse of {b^n}
  ]
 
 [;int_mult_power_rule For positive integer {n,m},
  What would {b^(n*m)} be?
  Reasoning from the solution, and @integer_power_rule
  {
   (b^n)^m
   = Pi__(m times) (Pi__(n times) b)
   = Pi__(m*n times) b
   = b^(m*n)
  }
  ]
 
 [What would {b^(1/n)} be?
  We have {
   b^(1/n * n) = b^1 = b
  }, also according to @int_mult_power_rule (doesn't apply here, but we'd like to use it) {
   b^(1/n * n) = (b^(1/n))^n = b
  }, so {
   b^(1/n) = root(n, b)
  }
  ]
 
 [The rules of root: suppose {x} is a positive real number (excluding 0),
  and {n} is a positive integer (including 0)
  { root(n, x) = y } such that { y^n = x }
  
  [;root_mult_decompose What is {root(n*m, x)}?
   Let { r = root(n*m, x) }
   We have { r^(n*m) = x }
   so { (r^n)^m = x }
   So {r^n = root(m, x)}, so {r = root(n, root(m, x))}
   ]
  
  [;root_canceling Prove that {root(n m, b^m) = root(n, b)}
   {root(n m, b^m) = root(n, root(m, b^m)) = root(n, b)}
   because {root(m, b^m) = b}
   ]
  
  [;root_same_mult Prove that
   {root(n, b) * root(n, c) = root(n, b c)}
   Let {s = root(n, b); t = root(n, c)}
   We have {s^n = b; t^n = c}
   So {b c = s^n t^n = (s t)^n}, meaning {s t = root(n, b c)} #qed
   ]
  
  [#done ;root_power_swap If {p,q} are integers, {q > 0}, prove that
   {root(q, x^p) = (root(q, x))^p}
   
   Set {y = root(q, x)}, we're trying to prove
   {root(q, x^p) = y^p}
   or {(y^p)^q = x^p}
   We have {
    y^q = x
    -> (y^q)^p = x^p
    -> (y^p)^q = x^p
   }
   which is what we're trying to prove, so #qed
   ]
  ]
 
 [;rational_exponent We have a definition of exponentiation
  when the exponent is a rational number {b^(n/m) = root(m, b^n)}
  
  [We try to prove the ;add_power_rule that {
    b^(n1 / m1) * b^(n2 / m2)
    = b^(n1 / m1 + n2 / m2)
   }
   
   Starting from the lhs {
    b^(n1 / m1) * b^(n2 / m2)
    = root(m1, b^n1) * root(m2, b^n2)
    // applying @root_canceling
    = root(m1 m2, b^(n1 m2)) * root(m1 m2, b^(n2 m1))
    // applying @root_same_mult
    = root(m1 m2, b^(n1 m2) b^(n2 m1))
    // NOTE @integer_add_power_rule here,
    = root(m1 m2, b^(n1 m2 + n2 m1))
    = b^(n1 / m1 + n2 / m2)
   }, which is the rhs, so #qed
   ]
  
  [#done Next we try to prove the ;mult_power_rule that {
    (b^(n1 / m1))^(n2 / m2) = b^(frac(n1 n2, m1 m2))
   }
   
   Starting from the lhs {
    (b^(n1 / m1))^(n2 / m2)
    = root(m2, (root(m1, b^n1))^n2)
    // According to @root_power_swap
    = root(m2, root(m1, b^(n1 n2)))
    // According to @root_mult_decompose
    = root(m1 m2, b^(n1 n2))
    = b^(frac(n1 n2, m1 m2))
   }, which is the rhs, so #qed
   ]
  ]
 ]

[The logistic function
 {
  sigma(x)
  = frac(1, 1 + e^(-x))
  // Using @negation_of_power
  = frac(e^x, 1 + e^x)
  = 1 - frac(1, 1 + e^x)
  = 1 - sigma(-x)
 }
 The last identity means that it is kinda symmetric
 
 When {x -> +infty}, {e^(-x)} goes to 0, {sigma(x)} goes to 1,
 the symmetric property deduces that, when {x -> -infty}, {sigma(x)} goes to 0
 
 [The hyperbolic tangent
  {tanh(x) = frac(e^x - e^(-x), e^x + e^(-x))}
  we have {tanh(-x) = -tanh(x)}
  ]
 ]

[Andrej Karpathy's [the spelled out intro to neural networks and backpropagation]
 [A neuron
  {
   o = tanh(n)
   = tanh(w1 x1 + w2 x2 + b)
   = tanh(-6 + b)
  }
  
  What is the gradient of {n}?
  {frac(d o, d n) = frac(d tanh(n), d n) = 1 - tanh^2(n)
   = 1 - o^2 = 1/2}
  
  What is the gradient of the bias {b}?
  {frac(d o, d b) = frac(d o, d n) frac(d n, d b)
   = frac(d o, d n) = 1/2}
  
  Similarly, {grad(w1 x1) = grad(w2 x2) = 1/2}
  Finally, {grad(w1) = 1/2 x1}, {grad(w2) = 1/2 x2}
  
  [todo How do we solve the backpropagation problem?
   [Well, we certainly could do it recursively]
   ]
  ]
 ]
